  "query_preview": "/Through Tuesday/...\nIssued at 1228 PM MDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nGOES-18 satellite data early this afternoon show that robust \nconvective initiation is ongoing across the Front Range Foothills.\nRadar estimates and precipitation gauges suggest that some of \nthese initial storms have still been efficient rainfall producers \nacross the foothills... meaning that burn area flash flooding is \nstill a concern this afternoon.\n\nHowever, much like the past several days... initial convection \nhas struggled once making it into the urban corridor. Why is this?\nThere are several possible explanations. Despite a relatively\nfavorable environment (T/Td of 90/57 at DEN), ACARS soundings  \nstill maintain a subtle stable layer between roughly 750-675 mb. \nWhile there's still 1500-2000 J/kg MLCAPE in that sounding there's\nalso a relatively deep, weakly buoyant region above the LFC that \nwould be prone to some entrainment. With little/no synoptic \nascent through this layer, it would likely take larger, mature \nupdrafts to overcome this. It's possible additional CI attempts \nacross the Foothills will lead to more sustained convection across\nthe Denver metro, but it appears more likely that our favored \nterrain (northern Front Range and Palmer Divide) will see the \nbulk of the rainfall this afternoon, with gradual upscale growth \ntowards east of the I-25 corridor later this afternoon and \nevening.\n\nAs that cluster of convection shifts eastward, the increased \nprecipitable water and deeper warm cloud layer depths would favor \nthe potential for some brief but rather heavy rainfall rates this \nevening. Recent REFS 3hr PMM QPF hints at as much as 3-5\" of \nrainfall this evening in Washington county... with broad support \nfrom today's HREF members as well. I saw no reason to adjust the\nexisting Flood Watch across the plains, which will remain in\neffect from 3-10 PM. As the midnight shift noted, there is still a\nslim possibility that this cluster of storms eventually sends some\noutflow back towards the metro, which would provide another\nopportunity for some showers/storms that would be capable of\nproducing some heavy rain.\n\nWednesday looks to be the last day of well above normal moisture,\nthough similar concerns remain about the coverage of storms across\nthe region. Overall we expect a fairly similar convective setup\ncompared to today, with perhaps a bit more coverage along/west of\nI-25. Storm chances will be dictated by the amount of moisture\nreturn tonight/tomorrow morning. Assuming we do see low/mid 60s\ndew points across the urban corridor tomorrow morning, there would\nbe some heavy rain potential once again.\n\nThe latter half of the week will feature a quick drying trend,\nwith warmer temperatures likely returning by the weekend. Chances\nfor showers/storms will continue for Thursday but should quickly\ndiminish by Saturday or Sunday as a 600 dam ridge at 500mb\ndevelops near the Four Corners region. Guidance hints that we\ncould see a return of upper 90s/low 100s by the end of the\nweekend, which then persist through early next week.",
